[task-01] The shortest opening the client marks as reusable follows Anthropic's current
    documented minimum for each model: 512 tokens for Claude Sonnet 5.5, Opus 5.5 and
    Haiku 5.5. The module documentation says that Anthropic ignores a breakpoint below
    the minimum, rather than that it rejects one.
    -> min-reusable-prefix-512-tokens  [boundary/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The shortest opening (prefix) the client marks as reusable (cache breakpoint) is
       512 tokens for each of Claude Sonnet 5.5, Opus 5.5 and Haiku 5.5, matching
       Anthropic's currently documented minimum for each model.
    -> module-doc-states-anthropic-ignores-sub-minimum-breakpoint  [docs_config/explicit, code_only]
       The module documentation states that Anthropic ignores a cache breakpoint placed
       below the model's minimum token count, rather than stating that it rejects such a
       breakpoint.
       why code_only: The obligation concerns the wording of the module documentation,
       which a reader confirms by inspecting the docstring; no runtime behavior exists
       for a test to exercise.

[task-02] In the mutation descriptor stage, a region's numbered source sits in the
    reusable opening, so calls for defects in the same region share it instead of each
    repeating it.
    -> region-source-in-shared-opening  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       In the mutation descriptor stage, a region's numbered source is placed in the
       reusable opening of the prompt, so that calls for defects in the same region
       share it instead of each call repeating it.

[task-03] A run can opt in to sending its calls to Claude models through the Message
    Batches API. The setting belongs to the run's configuration and is off by default,
    so `acceptance check` and `acceptance decompose` keep calling the model directly.
    Benchmark scoring and experiment scripts can switch it on.
    -> batch-api-opt-in  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A run can opt in to sending its calls to Claude models through the Message
       Batches API; when opted in, the run's Claude model calls are submitted via the
       Message Batches API.
    -> batch-setting-in-run-configuration  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The opt-in setting for sending a run's calls to Claude models through the Message
       Batches API is held as part of the run's configuration, so that each run carries
       its own value of the setting.
    -> batch-api-opt-in-off-by-default  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The run configuration setting that opts a run into sending its Claude model calls
       through the Message Batches API defaults to off, so a run that does not set it
       calls the model directly.
    -> check-decompose-call-model-directly  [invariant/explicit, code_and_tests]
       By default, `acceptance check` and `acceptance decompose` call the model
       directly, not through the Message Batches API; the batch opt-in being off by
       default leaves their direct-call behavior in place.
    -> batch-opt-in-switchable-by-scripts  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       Benchmark scoring scripts and experiment scripts can enable the Message Batches
       API opt-in setting for a run through the run's configuration, so that their
       Claude model calls are sent via the Message Batches API.

[task-04] How a call was sent does not change what was asked: a batched request is
    recorded under the same key as the same request sent directly, and its recording
    replays with no live call. The recording notes that it was sent through a batch. Two
    recorded runs over the same input still produce byte-identical review state
    whichever way their calls were sent.
    -> batched-request-same-recording-key  [invariant/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A request sent through the batch path is recorded under the same key as the
       identical request sent directly, so the recording key does not depend on the send
       method.
    -> batched-recording-replays-offline  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A recording made from a batched request replays without issuing any live call.
    -> recording-notes-batch-origin  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       When a call is sent through a batch, its recording carries a note indicating that
       it was sent through a batch.
    -> byte-identical-review-state-across-send-modes  [invariant/explicit, code_and_tests]
       Two recorded runs over the same input produce byte-identical review state
       regardless of whether their calls were sent directly or through a batch.

